(where native compensation WOULD have put the tool - as a vector, for the ops)
(that compensate in CAM by offsetting a straight wall analytically instead of)
(asking the interpreter for G41.1/G42.1)
()
(A straight wall's compensated path is the wall shifted by a CONSTANT vector, so)
(one addition to each endpoint reproduces exactly what native comp computes -)
(which is the definition of correct here, because native comp is already proven)
(per op by the tangency verifier. For a straight wall the two modes should)
(therefore land on identical coordinates, and that is the acceptance test.)
()
(The convention below was measured with rs274, not derived on paper. With u the)
(unit travel direction in Z and radius:)
(   G42 offsets along  u_x, -u_z   and G41 along  -u_x, u_z)
(Verified two ways: a -Z cut at radius 40 came out at 39.6 under G41.1 and 40.4)
(under G42.1. A -X face at Z-5 came out at Z-4.6 under G41.1 and Z-5.4 under)
(G42.1 - both with D0.8 L0, so the shift is the pure normal with no orientation)
(term. Getting this sign backwards puts the wall a full nose diameter out.)
()
(The orientation term is LinuxCNC's own lathe_shapes table, the same one)
(lathe_sections.NOSE_OFFSET carries. It is a RAW vector: entries 1-4 have)
(magnitude R*SQRT2 because the imaginary sharp tip of a 90 degree insert)
(corner sits SQRT2*R from the round nose centre. Normalising it to R would)
(mis-place the tool - CLAUDE.md flags this specifically.)
()
(CALL side[1] dz[2] dx[3] extra_r[4])
(side    : 41 or 42, the side the op would have compensated on)
(dz, dx  : travel direction along the wall - any length, normalised here.)
(          dx in RADIUS units, not as an X word)
(extra_r : per-side allowance folded in, 0 for a finish wall)
(Sets #<_tip_off_z> / #<_tip_off_x>, in radius units, to ADD to a programmed)
(point. Both are 0 when there is no nose to compensate, so a caller can apply)
(them unconditionally and get its original path back.)
o<tip_comp_vec> sub
	#<tv_side>  = #1
	#<tv_dz>    = #2
	#<tv_dx>    = #3
	#<tv_extra> = #4

	#<_tip_off_z> = 0
	#<_tip_off_x> = 0

	#<tv_len> = SQRT[[#<tv_dz> * #<tv_dz>] + [#<tv_dx> * #<tv_dx>]]
	o<tv_deg> if [#<tv_len> LT 0.000001]
		(a zero-length wall has no direction to take a normal from)
		o<tip_comp_vec> return
	o<tv_deg> endif

	#<tv_r> = [#<_tip_cam_r> + #<tv_extra>]
	o<tv_none> if [#<tv_r> LT 0.000001]
		o<tip_comp_vec> return
	o<tv_none> endif

	#<tv_uz> = [#<tv_dz> / #<tv_len>]
	#<tv_ux> = [#<tv_dx> / #<tv_len>]

	(the normal, on the side the op compensates to)
	o<tv_sd> if [#<tv_side> EQ 42]
		#<tv_nz> = #<tv_ux>
		#<tv_nx> = [-#<tv_uz>]
	o<tv_sd> else
		#<tv_nz> = [-#<tv_ux>]
		#<tv_nx> = #<tv_uz>
	o<tv_sd> endif

	(orientation offset in Z and radius, from LinuxCNC's lathe_shapes. That)
	(table lists X then Z, so each pair is written the other way round here)
	#<tv_oz> = 0
	#<tv_ox> = 0
	o<tv_or> if [#<_tip_cam_l> EQ 1]
		#<tv_oz> = -1
		#<tv_ox> = 1
	o<tv_or> elseif [#<_tip_cam_l> EQ 2]
		#<tv_oz> = 1
		#<tv_ox> = 1
	o<tv_or> elseif [#<_tip_cam_l> EQ 3]
		#<tv_oz> = 1
		#<tv_ox> = -1
	o<tv_or> elseif [#<_tip_cam_l> EQ 4]
		#<tv_oz> = -1
		#<tv_ox> = -1
	o<tv_or> elseif [#<_tip_cam_l> EQ 5]
		#<tv_oz> = -1
		#<tv_ox> = 0
	o<tv_or> elseif [#<_tip_cam_l> EQ 6]
		#<tv_oz> = 0
		#<tv_ox> = 1
	o<tv_or> elseif [#<_tip_cam_l> EQ 7]
		#<tv_oz> = 1
		#<tv_ox> = 0
	o<tv_or> elseif [#<_tip_cam_l> EQ 8]
		#<tv_oz> = 0
		#<tv_ox> = -1
	o<tv_or> endif

	(control point = wall + half_D*normal - R*orientation, half_D = R + extra)
	#<_tip_off_z> = [[#<tv_r> * #<tv_nz>] - [#<_tip_cam_r> * #<tv_oz>]]
	#<_tip_off_x> = [[#<tv_r> * #<tv_nx>] - [#<_tip_cam_r> * #<tv_ox>]]
o<tip_comp_vec> endsub
